v-404` supplies its own padding. Good.\n\nLet me read those chunks now. Also verify no existing 404.php at theme root, and check `grep -n \"error404\" style.css` for any existing 404 rules.", "reasoning_details": "[{\"type\": \"reasoning.text\", \"text\": \"I have a good picture now. Let me also check:\\n1. The `.sv-shop-banner` (shop banner) for context of a page-hero on subpages \\u2014 maybe I can use a similar visual band at the top of 404. Let me grep \\\"sv-shop\\\" in style.css.\\n2. Check whether search form styling exists anywhere (sv-orders-filter input styles at 707-720) \\u2014 I can craft `.sv-404__form input[type=\\\"search\\\"]` consistent with input language. Let me check global input styles (e.g., `input[type=\\\"text\\\"]` in the theme) for the general input language: 48px height, borders, etc.\\n\\nLet me grep for `input[type=` more thoroughly (I only saw a few). Also check `.sv-account-form input` etc. Let me search \\\"input\\\" in style.css with limit to find the \\\"input dili\\\" section.\\n\\nAlso, I want to double check where the 404 markup should go and how the theme renders title on 404 \\u2014 body class error404. Also confirm no `404.php` currently in the theme dir: earlier `ls woocommerce/` showed no 404.php; let me ls the theme root.\\n\\nNext steps plan:\\n1. ls theme root to confirm no 404.php.\\n2. grep \\\"sv-shop-banner|banner\\\" in style.css + functions.php to see the shop banner design (optional).\\n3. grep input styles.\\n\\nThen write the files.\\n\\nDesign for 404.php:\\n\\n```php\\n<?php\\n/**\\n * 404 \\u2014 sayfa bulunamad\\u0131 \\u015fablonu (P83).\\n * Klasik mimari: get_header()/get_footer() \\u2014 header/footer/tek CSS kural\\u0131 korunur.\\n */\\nif ( ! defined( 'ABSPATH' ) ) { exit; }\\nget_header();\\n?>\\n<main class=\\\"sv-404\\\" id=\\\"sv-404\\\">\\n\\t<div class=\\\"sv-404__inner\\\">\\n\\t\\t<p class=\\\"sv-404__code\\\" aria-hidden=\\\"true\\\">404</p>\\n\\t\\t<h1 class=\\\"sv-404__title\\\">Arad\\u0131\\u011f\\u0131n\\u0131z sayfay\\u0131 bulamad\\u0131k</h1>\\n\\t\\t<p class=\\\"sv-404__text\\\">Ba\\u011flant\\u0131 ta\\u015f\\u0131nm\\u0131\\u015f veya adres yanl\\u0131\\u015f yaz\\u0131lm\\u0131\\u015f olabilir. A\\u015fa\\u011f\\u0131dan arayabilir ya da ma\\u011fazaya d\\u00f6nebilirsiniz.</p>\\n\\n\\t\\t<form role=\\\"search\\\" method=\\\"get\\\" class=\\\"sv-404__form\\\" action=\\\"<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/' ) ); ?>\\\">\\n\\t\\t\\t<label class=\\\"screen-reader-text\\\" for=\\\"sv-404-search\\\">Sitede ara</label>\\n\\t\\t\\t<input type=\\\"search\\\" id=\\\"sv-404-search\\\" class=\\\"sv-404__input\\\" name=\\\"s\\\" value=\\\"<?php echo esc_attr( get_search_query() ); ?>\\\" placeholder=\\\"\\u00dcr\\u00fcn veya sayfa ara\\u2026\\\">\\n\\t\\t\\t<button type=\\\"submit\\\" class=\\\"sv-404__submit\\\">Ara</button>\\n\\t\\t</form>\\n\\n\\t\\t<div class=\\\"sv-404__actions\\\">\\n\\t\\t\\t<a class=\\\"sv-404__btn sv-404__btn--primary\\\" href=\\\"<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/' ) ); ?>\\\">Ana sayfaya d\\u00f6n</a>\\n\\t\\t\\t<a class=\\\"sv-404__btn sv-404__btn--ghost\\\" href=\\\"<?php echo esc_url( function_exists( 'wc_get_page_permalink' ) ? wc_get_page_permalink( 'shop' ) : home_url( '/shop/' ) ); ?>\\\">Ma\\u011fazaya git</a>\\n\\t\\t</div>\\n\\t</div>\\n</main>\\n<?php get_footer(); ?>\\n```\\n\\nWait \\u2014 \\\"screen-reader-text\\\" class: does the theme define it? WP core doesn't define `.screen-reader-text` in a classic non-block theme unless the theme does. header.php uses class \\\"skip-link screen-reader-text\\\". Let me check style.css for `.screen-reader-text`. If missing, the label would be visible. Let me grep.\\n\\nAlso decide about including 3 featured products. Considering \\\"bo\\u015f alan kalmad\\u0131\\u011f\\u0131n\\u0131n kan\\u0131t\\u0131\\\" and the optional item, I'll consider adding \\\"T\\u00fcm koleksiyon\\\" link... Actually \\\"Ma\\u011fazaya git\\\" covers it. Hmm, but there's a real benefit: showing 3 products makes 404 page useful and fills the space visually (quiet luxury stores often show best-sellers). The pack says optional; only with existing WooCommerce queries and wp_reset_postdata if a query is added.\\n\\nI lean towards including featured products via a proper WP_Query + wc_get_template_part loop, because:\\n- It reuses the global card design automatically (P37 rule: product lists get the design automatically).\\n- wp_reset_postdata() required \\u2014 I'll do it.\\n- Only on 404 pages, one query.\\n\\nBut risk: with only 2 products in the store, `ul.products` 2-col grid with 2 items = fine.\\n\\nYet, hmm \\u2014 is it \\\"bozmadan performans\\\"? One WP_Query on a 404 page: fine.\\n\\nHowever, there's an important nuance: the `[products]` shortcode / wc loop uses `wc_set_loop_prop` for columns. If I call `woocommerce_product_loop_start()`, Woo outputs `<ul class=\\\"products columns-...\\\">`. The theme CSS overrides `ul.products` grid to 2 columns repeat(2, minmax(0,1fr)) globally and 1 col at 480. So columns props don't matter much.\\n\\nLet me weigh simplicity: The pack's core acceptance is: heading + search + two buttons; CSS scoped; empty area filled. Adding products adds complexity and risk with no acceptance gain. The pack explicitly marks it optional. The stronger engineering choice here is to keep it minimal and robust \\u2014 but a 404 with a bit of content... The heading+search+buttons already fills the area.\\n\\nI'll include featured/on-sale products? Let me think about the visual: a quiet luxury minimal 404 with big serif title, search, two buttons centered \\u2014 that's clean and consistent with the design system. I'll skip the product loop to minimize risk (query + template part + potential layout issues on a page type that isn't tested by other flows). Wait, but \\\"en \\u00e7ok satan/\\u00f6ne \\u00e7\\u0131kan 3 \\u00fcr\\u00fcn ya da 'T\\u00fcm koleksiyon' ba\\u011flant\\u0131s\\u0131\\\" \\u2014 the \\\"ya da\\\" gives the option of the