 consideration: `body.error404` \\u2014 does the theme add anything special? No error404 rules \\u2192 good; the .sv-404 provides its own spacing.\\n\\nOld Woo CSS might add `.woocommerce-...`? No.\\n\\nThe 404 template \\u2014 potential \\\"no .woocommerce wrapper\\\" issue: Woo's `.woocommerce` wrapper is added by Woo templates on shop pages, not 404. Our markup is self-contained.\\n\\nNow `get_search_query()` \\u2014 hmm, use `esc_attr( get_search_query() )` \\u2014 that function escapes by default... `get_search_query()` returns esc_attr'ed by default? Actually `get_search_query( $escaped = true )` escapes with esc_attr by default. Double escaping is harmless but let me just use `get_search_query()` since it's already escaped (like WP's own searchform). Actually WP core searchform.php uses `get_search_query()` directly. To be safe/explicit I'll use `esc_attr( get_search_query() )` \\u2014 wait, that double-escapes on search results page (rare case: the 404 page submitted with `s` param \\u2192 URL /?s=query \\u2192 that goes to search results, not 404). So value is almost always empty. Fine either way. I'll use `esc_attr( get_search_query( false ) )`? Hmm, simpler: `get_search_query()`. Let me check what the theme uses for its search input (orders.php uses esc_attr on a GET filter). I'll use `esc_attr( get_search_query() )` \\u2014 harmless.\\n\\nHmm wait, actually there's an important subtlety: WP's `get_search_query()` when the request is a 404 might return the query string. If someone visits /404-test-xyz/, `s` isn't set \\u2192 empty. OK.\\n\\nNow \\u2014 the products loop: I need to be careful about `wc_get_products` availability: on 404 WooCommerce is active (site store). Use `function_exists('wc_get_products')` guard to degrade gracefully. And fallback: if no featured \\u2192 pick latest visible products.\\n\\nImplementation detail for featured\\u2192latest:\\n\\n```php\\n$sv404_ids = wc_get_products( array(\\n\\t'status'     => 'publish',\\n\\t'limit'      => 3,\\n\\t'featured'   => true,\\n\\t'visibility' => 'catalog',\\n\\t'return'     => 'ids',\\n\\t'orderby'    => 'date',\\n\\t'order'      => 'DESC',\\n) );\\nif ( empty( $sv404_ids ) ) {\\n\\t$sv404_ids = wc_get_products( array(\\n\\t\\t'status'     => 'publish',\\n\\t\\t'limit'      => 3,\\n\\t\\t'visibility' => 'catalog',\\n\\t\\t'return'     => 'ids',\\n\\t\\t'orderby'    => 'date',\\n\\t\\t'order'      => 'DESC',\\n\\t) );\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHmm \\u2014 'visibility' => 'catalog' requires the 'woocommerce_product_visibility' arg support; supported by WC_Product_Query. Yes.\\n\\nThen:\\n\\n```php\\nif ( ! empty( $sv404_ids ) ) :\\n\\t$sv404_query = new WP_Query( array(\\n\\t\\t'post_type'           => 'product',\\n\\t\\t'post_status'         => 'publish',\\n\\t\\t'post__in'            => $sv404_ids,\\n\\t\\t'orderby'             => 'post__in',\\n\\t\\t'posts_per_page'      => 3,\\n\\t\\t'no_found_rows'       => true,\\n\\t\\t'ignore_sticky_posts' => true,\\n\\t\\t'fields'              => 'ids'?  -- no, need full posts for template\\n\\t) );\\n\\tif ( $sv404_query->have_posts() ) : ?>\\n\\t\\t<section class=\\\"sv-404__featured\\\" aria-labelledby=\\\"sv-404-featured-title\\\">\\n\\t\\t\\t<h2 class=\\\"sv-404__featured-title\\\" id=\\\"sv-404-featured-title\\\"><?php esc_html_e( '\\u00d6ne \\u00c7\\u0131kanlar', 'sutre' ); ?></h2>\\n\\t\\t\\t<?php\\n\\t\\t\\twoocommerce_product_loop_start();\\n\\t\\t\\twhile ( $sv404_query->have_posts() ) :\\n\\t\\t\\t\\t$sv404_query->the_post();\\n\\t\\t\\t\\twc_get_template_part( 'content', 'product' );\\n\\t\\t\\tendwhile;\\n\\t\\t\\twoocommerce_product_loop_end();\\n\\t\\t\\twp_reset_postdata();\\n\\t\\t\\t?>\\n\\t\\t</section>\\n\\t<?php endif; wp_reset_postdata(); endif;\\n```\\n\\nNeed to wrap `woocommerce_product_loop_start/end` + `wc_get_template_part` in function_exists guards too (if Woo disabled, skip). Actually the whole featured section is inside `if ( function_exists('wc_get_products') )`.\\n\\nHmm \\u2014 `woocomme